Tyler:

If you have entered your data as if birds were in clusters when indeed
they were not in clusters, then your data are not accurately represented
to Distance.  That is reason enough to re-enter the data.  However you
would certainly find yourself in difficulty were you to consider using
cluster size as a covariate influencing the detection function because
your cluster sizes are incorrect.

It seems there is no easy computational solution to reorganising your
data; you will need to go back to the data sheets to determine which
observations were indeed clusters and which observations were of singles
combined into clusters at time of data entry.

On 24/12/2010 16:27, Tyler Beck wrote:
> > I am trying to analyze point transect data from a multi-bird species study and have a lot of questions but I'll start with a basic one.  The data was entered into the database as clusters in distance bins (0-10m,25m,50m,100m,150m,200m).  However, the observations were not necessarily in clusters and may have been individuals in different locations.  Is it preferred that I change my data structure to individuals or is it ok that I leave them entered as cluster?  If I must change my data structure does anybody have an effecient method to do so either in Excel, SAS or Distance?
