MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

As Eric points out, you can't use the mcds engine of Distance with
cluster size as a covariate.  However, you can do a 'conventional
distance sampling' (cds) analysis.  e.g. for your example of 30+2 birds,
you can enter say the midpoint of that distance interval for each of 32
individual sightings, then analyse as grouped distances, entering the
cutpoints in the data filter.  Strictly, this should have been five
separate sightings, with cluster sizes of 10, 10, 10, 1 and 1.  Treating
this as 32 sightings is a strong violation of the independence
assumption.  However, cds methods are very robust to such violation.
Model selection tools and goodness-of-fit tests are more sensitive to
this failure though, so care should be taken not to overfit the distance
data.  See for example pp75-76, 127, and 167-170 of the 2001 distance
sampling book.

Steve Buckland

